const posts=[
 {
  name:"Why MCP Makes Software Feel Alive",
  grid:grid({
   eyebrow:"PROTOCOLS & POSSIBILITIES",
   heading:"Why MCP makes software feel alive",
   introduction:"MCP gives an AI assistant a clear, inspectable way to work with the tools and data already living on your machine.",
   imageId:"8e8abce6-9275-4183-8028-9f726ccf4df8",
   alt:"Colourful source code filling a computer screen",
   credit:"Photo by Chris Ried on Unsplash",
   markup:`<p>Most software tools wait for us to learn their menus, APIs, and conventions. The Model Context Protocol flips that relationship around: it gives an assistant a structured map of what a system can do, then lets the assistant combine those capabilities around an outcome.</p><h2>A shared language for useful work</h2><p>MCP tools are explicit. A content command describes the fields it accepts. A read operation returns structured data. An error can explain exactly which assumption failed. That shared language turns a vague request into a sequence of actions that both people and software can inspect.</p><p>On this site, the protocol did more than answer questions. It inspected the existing Umbraco structure, created element types, configured a Block Grid, imported local media, and published this article. Each step used the same management surface an editor or integration would trust.</p><h2>Power with boundaries</h2><p>The best automation is not magic. It is powerful because its boundaries are visible. MCP makes allowed operations discoverable, encourages typed inputs, and keeps the host application in charge of validation and permissions.</p><blockquote><p>The exciting part is not that an assistant can click faster. It is that intent can meet a well-defined capability without losing the rules of the system.</p></blockquote><h2>Composable by design</h2><p>A small tool can become part of a much larger workflow. Schema inspection, media upload, content creation, and verification remain separate capabilities, but MCP lets them cooperate. That makes integrations easier to evolve and far easier to reason about than one giant opaque automation script.</p><p>When software can clearly describe what it knows and what it can do, it stops feeling like a collection of closed boxes. It starts feeling like a living workspace.</p>`
  })
 },
 {
  name:"Local MCP Is a Developer Superpower",
  grid:grid({
   eyebrow:"LOCAL-FIRST WORKFLOWS",
   heading:"Local MCP is a developer superpower",
   introduction:"A local MCP server collapses the distance between an idea, the real application, and a verified result—without skipping the project’s rules.",
   imageId:"c31ace9e-7c01-4828-8277-7888bc162fbb",
   alt:"Two people fitting wooden puzzle pieces together",
   credit:"Photo by Vardan Papikyan on Unsplash",
   markup:`<p>Fast feedback changes how we build. When an assistant can talk to the local application through MCP, it can inspect the real schema, use the real validation pipeline, and immediately read its work back. That is a much richer loop than generating a pile of files and hoping they line up later.</p><h2>The application stays the source of truth</h2><p>This blog was created through Umbraco itself. Document types live in the prescribed folders. Media sits inside a media folder. Pages use the project’s root repository and routing convention. The Block Grid payloads were accepted, published, and read back by the running CMS.</p><p>That matters because local automation should reinforce architecture rather than route around it. The server owns identifiers, validation, persistence, and publishing state. The assistant orchestrates those capabilities while the application remains authoritative.</p><h2>Feedback arrives while context is fresh</h2><p>A local MCP loop can catch mistakes at the point they happen: a missing required value, an invalid content type, a malformed block key, or a write that did not survive serialization. Each read-back narrows uncertainty before the next step begins.</p><ul><li>Inspect before creating.</li><li>Use typed tools instead of guessing endpoints.</li><li>Read structural writes back immediately.</li><li>Build rendering only after the content model is settled.</li></ul><h2>Automation that respects craft</h2><p>Speed is useful, but the deeper win is continuity. The same workflow can honor naming, folders, rendering conventions, image handling, and deployment artifacts while still moving quickly. The protocol provides leverage without erasing the decisions that make a codebase coherent.</p><p>That combination—local context, explicit capabilities, and rapid verification—is why MCP feels less like a shortcut and more like a genuine developer superpower.</p>`
  })
 }
];
